# Cloudflare Pages serves this project's own modules with
# `cache-control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate`, and must-revalidate
# only bites once a response is stale, so for four hours after a deploy a
# browser that already had the page keeps running the previous JavaScript
# without asking. That made a fix that was deployed and verified in production
# invisible to the person who reported the bug.
#
# Measured: Pages will not go below that floor. `Cache-Control: no-cache` here
# came back as `max-age=14400`, and `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` was
# ignored outright. Values above the floor are honored, which is why vendor/
# works. The mitigation therefore lives in the page: index.html is served with
# max-age=0, so it always carries the current build stamp, and app.js compares
# that stamp with the one baked into itself and says so when they differ.
#
# Third-party code changes only when it is deliberately upgraded, so it keeps a
# week rather than four hours.

/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
